WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.233
[rifle shot]

00:00:04.233 --> 00:00:05.366
[woman squeals]
[man screams]

00:00:05.366 --> 00:00:07.007
[man screams]

00:00:08.800 --> 00:00:10.495
Nicholas Irving: Not happening.

00:00:10.495 --> 00:00:11.870
To the left?

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:13.580
Good to go?

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:15.000
My name's Nicholas Irving.

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:16.980
I'm a former 3rd Ranger Battalion

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:18.720
special operations sniper

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:21.790
and currently a New York
Times best-selling author.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:24.170
I spent six years in special operations,

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:28.000
and I'm known as The Reaper for 33 kills

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:29.720
in about a three-month time span

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.440
in Helmand Province, Afghanistan.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:32.450
Today we're gonna be looking at

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.517
a few sniper scenes in movies.

00:00:48.710 --> 00:00:50.512
No one ever really does that.

00:00:55.967 --> 00:00:56.925
[rifle shot]

00:00:58.367 --> 00:00:59.870
[window shatters]

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.233
Ah. No.

00:01:03.550 --> 00:01:06.350
His use of the equipment
was really, really good.

00:01:06.350 --> 00:01:09.250
He has his, you know, his pad to lay on,

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:11.620
he has his range finder,

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:13.030
he can measure the wind,

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:13.958
barometric pressure,

00:01:13.958 --> 00:01:15.990
the altitude, density altitude,

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:16.940
his DOPE book.

00:01:16.940 --> 00:01:19.230
The word DOPE is an acronym for snipers,

00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:22.720
and it actually stands for
data on previous engagements.

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:24.930
So he can go back and
refer to all these things

00:01:24.930 --> 00:01:26.920
that he's engaged before that

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.510
and have, like, some good reference point.

00:01:29.510 --> 00:01:32.680
He applied all of his DOPE in
his data to the scope itself.

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:34.570
I rarely see that in
movies, where, you know,

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:36.767
he applies the elevation and the windage.

00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.370
The impossible portion
about this whole thing

00:01:39.370 --> 00:01:41.480
was the actual shot itself.

00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:44.500
The train is moving way too fast.

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:47.160
You have to, you know,
defeat gravity in some ways.

00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.130
So you're aiming up, and
the bullet's dropping

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.110
almost down at that distance.

00:01:51.110 --> 00:01:52.740
And shooting through glass,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:53.950
panes of glass,

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:57.018
angled glass and different types of glass

00:01:57.018 --> 00:02:00.060
may deflect and alter the bullet's flight.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.640
You'd have to be

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.180
one really, really,
really, really lucky guy,

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:08.670
or, after that, get up
and go walk on water.

00:02:08.670 --> 00:02:09.910
But now that I see it,

00:02:09.910 --> 00:02:11.240
I almost wanna simulate it,

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:12.190
but it's kind of, you know,

00:02:12.190 --> 00:02:13.930
it'd be almost impossible to simulate.

00:02:13.930 --> 00:02:16.998
I can't find a target moving
at a bullet-train speed.

00:02:16.998 --> 00:02:20.573
Yeah, so this is a very basic

00:02:20.573 --> 00:02:22.990
bolt-action rifle. Remington.

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:25.340
It shoots a 6.5 [millimeter]
Creedmoor round.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:26.840
Very, very fast rounds.

00:02:26.840 --> 00:02:29.062
Small caliber, but I use it

00:02:29.062 --> 00:02:31.210
essentially because of the speed of it.

00:02:31.210 --> 00:02:32.720
I use it to defeat the wind.

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:34.275
If there's a lot of wind or

00:02:34.275 --> 00:02:35.983
shifty winds or anything like that,

00:02:35.983 --> 00:02:38.220
it's a good bullet because
of the velocity of it,

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:39.320
how fast it's moving.

00:02:39.320 --> 00:02:40.500
He had a suppressant on his.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:41.333
I don't have one.

00:02:41.333 --> 00:02:44.000
But for, you know, overseas
work and stuff like that,

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:46.560
if I had to make a one shot, one kill,

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.550
I would probably take something

00:02:48.550 --> 00:02:52.000
along the lines of a bolt-action rifle.

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:53.340
Then you have your bipod legs,

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.090
and that's just for,
you know, stabilization.

00:02:56.090 --> 00:02:57.205
Have a scope,

00:02:57.205 --> 00:03:00.970
and of course you have
your trigger and buttstock.

00:03:00.970 --> 00:03:03.160
There's not much to a sniper rifle.

00:03:03.160 --> 00:03:04.060
His eye position,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:05.976
his cheek weld, where you put your cheek

00:03:05.976 --> 00:03:07.840
on the rifle itself, where it rests,

00:03:07.840 --> 00:03:10.190
his was pretty much perfect on his.

00:03:10.190 --> 00:03:11.500
You'll see in movies sometimes

00:03:11.500 --> 00:03:13.491
the guy will have his eye pressed

00:03:13.491 --> 00:03:14.641
all the way up on the scope,

00:03:14.641 --> 00:03:17.360
and, you know, unless
you want a huge black eye

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:19.712
and a nice, you know, bloody eyebrow,

00:03:19.712 --> 00:03:20.740
you wouldn't do that.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.190
I keep both eyes open.

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:23.999
That way, I can use my peripheral vision

00:03:23.999 --> 00:03:26.950
to, you know, keep my
situational awareness.

00:03:26.950 --> 00:03:29.290
Good body position behind the gun.

00:03:29.290 --> 00:03:31.410
You want your body to be, you know,

00:03:31.410 --> 00:03:33.390
almost perfectly aligned with the gun.

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:35.270
That way when the recoil comes back,

00:03:35.270 --> 00:03:36.970
you know, you're not bouncing off target,

00:03:36.970 --> 00:03:38.410
you can get a good follow-up shot.

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:40.090
Your skeletal structure

00:03:40.090 --> 00:03:42.360
is gonna support all that recoil.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:44.380
If you cant it off to the side,

00:03:44.380 --> 00:03:45.760
that gun is gonna hop.

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:47.600
So being perfectly behind the gun

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:49.540
as much as you possibly can be.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.192
That was a solid eight.

00:03:51.192 --> 00:03:52.390
Maybe even a nine.

00:03:52.390 --> 00:03:55.590
It was the shot itself that was a one.

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:57.681
I would never take that shot.

00:03:57.681 --> 00:03:59.083
Swofford: Range?

00:04:00.250 --> 00:04:01.731
Troy: 900 yards.

00:04:05.004 --> 00:04:06.029
Nicholas: That was good.

00:04:06.029 --> 00:04:07.171
That was really good.

00:04:09.370 --> 00:04:11.380
All the dialogue, you know,

00:04:11.380 --> 00:04:13.430
between spotter and shooter,

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.280
were really, really good.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:19.280
He kept his firing hand on
the rifle the entire time

00:04:19.280 --> 00:04:22.610
while he adjusted the
scope with his left hand.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.570
900 yards, though,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.120
definitely not gonna take a headshot.

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:28.150
And with that scope,

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.853
it wouldn't even look
like it's that close.

00:04:30.853 --> 00:04:32.427
It would look, you know,

00:04:32.427 --> 00:04:35.130
he would look way smaller than that.

00:04:35.130 --> 00:04:36.242
Troy: Permission to fire.

00:04:36.242 --> 00:04:37.075
[door bangs]

00:04:37.075 --> 00:04:38.861
Lincoln: What's the f---
frequency are you on?

00:04:38.861 --> 00:04:39.694
Swofford: Oh, f---.

00:04:39.694 --> 00:04:41.594
Lincoln: We got air. I'm calling it in.

00:04:41.594 --> 00:04:43.290
Swofford: We have
permission to take the shot.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.782
Lincoln: Tough break. You
were just gonna shoot one guy.

00:04:45.782 --> 00:04:49.410
Nicholas: I think where
it gets a little wacky

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:51.110
is where the guy busts into the room

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:53.318
and tells them to stand down.

00:04:53.318 --> 00:04:55.653
Snipers like to set up sniper hides,

00:04:55.653 --> 00:04:57.840
and they're essentially in a sniper hide,

00:04:57.840 --> 00:04:59.970
and there's booby traps and
stuff like that in there.

00:04:59.970 --> 00:05:01.360
Maybe that colonel,

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.270
or the guy who walked up the stairs,

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.450
may have hit a trip
wire or being, you know,

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:10.410
maybe engaged by the sniper team itself

00:05:10.410 --> 00:05:12.670
once he came into the room like that.

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:14.510
But if they have permission
from the colonel,

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:16.480
I would have taken the shot anyways.

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:18.010
I'm not too sure why this guy felt

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.280
that he had the authority to do that.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.110
I would've taken that shot.

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.226
Lincoln: This is dollar four-

00:05:23.226 --> 00:05:24.684
Troy: Goddamn it, he's dead anyway!

00:05:24.684 --> 00:05:26.490
Just let us f---ing do it!

00:05:26.490 --> 00:05:27.747
Nicholas: That is a moment.

00:05:27.747 --> 00:05:29.521
It's a weird moment,

00:05:29.521 --> 00:05:31.550
where you're looking through the scope

00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:33.160
and you see your target,

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:34.890
and you know for a fact that,

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.510
hey, I'm about to end this guy's life.

00:05:37.510 --> 00:05:42.390
It's a life-altering,
emotional-roller-coaster experience.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:44.560
I can see the emotion behind it.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:45.880
Scale from one to 10 for that one,

00:05:45.880 --> 00:05:50.319
I'm giving that one a nine.

00:05:50.319 --> 00:05:52.044
[crickets chirping]

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:58.580
Sgt. Maj.: Stop. Touch
that light-colored grass.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.050
Sniper at your feet.

00:06:00.050 --> 00:06:02.400
Nicholas: It's exactly like sniper school.

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:04.870
For one, that sniper scope is not,

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:06.160
that reticle inside the scope

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:09.200
is nothing like what you
would use in real life.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.683
Yeah, you usually have these
little mil dots on there.

00:06:11.683 --> 00:06:14.290
They're like little circles,
and you have four of them

00:06:14.290 --> 00:06:18.300
on each the horizontal
plane and the vertical plane

00:06:18.300 --> 00:06:20.480
inside the scope, and they're
used for measurements.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:21.520
So, let's say, for example,

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:24.510
if I had a target that was 40 inches tall,

00:06:24.510 --> 00:06:28.430
and I wanted to find out
how far away something is,

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:30.170
but what I would do is take the 40 inches

00:06:30.170 --> 00:06:32.230
and multiply it by a constant,

00:06:32.230 --> 00:06:34.290
which is 25.4,

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:37.573
and that comes out to 1,016.

00:06:37.573 --> 00:06:39.320
And I would take the mil dots,

00:06:39.320 --> 00:06:42.600
and, let's say, if the
target measures 2 mils high,

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:47.310
I would divide 1,016 by 2,

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:48.902
and that comes out to 508.

00:06:48.902 --> 00:06:52.960
So I add 508 yards on the
target, on my distance,

00:06:52.960 --> 00:06:55.594
and I would apply that
to the scope of my gun.

00:06:55.594 --> 00:06:57.380
[crickets chirping]

00:06:57.380 --> 00:06:58.880
[rifle shot]
[bullet ricochets]

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.085
Sgt. Maj.: Damn.

00:07:00.085 --> 00:07:01.100
Nicholas: Going to that trigger squeeze,

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:02.240
that was not really good.

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.240
You wanna use the pad of your finger.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.598
He kind of curled the
entire trigger finger.

00:07:06.598 --> 00:07:07.760
It's a precision weapon.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:09.820
You're looking at harmonics
and stuff like that,

00:07:09.820 --> 00:07:11.950
so imagine, like, a tuning fork,

00:07:11.950 --> 00:07:13.520
you want everything to be as smooth

00:07:13.520 --> 00:07:15.943
and less friction on the sides

00:07:15.943 --> 00:07:17.843
of that railing inside
the gun as possible.

00:07:17.843 --> 00:07:19.501
So having the trigger come straight back

00:07:19.501 --> 00:07:21.550
is what you really wanna look for,

00:07:21.550 --> 00:07:23.560
not so much of that curled trigger finger.

00:07:23.560 --> 00:07:25.780
And it's exactly how they
used it in sniper school,

00:07:25.780 --> 00:07:28.450
where the, you know, the instructor,

00:07:28.450 --> 00:07:30.130
he's telling the guys on the outside,

00:07:30.130 --> 00:07:33.100
"Hey, come in, sniper at
your feet, X, Y, and Z."

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:35.373
It's pretty much exactly how it works.

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.650
Sgt. Maj.: All right, you won this one.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.368
Come on out.

00:07:40.368 --> 00:07:41.283
[grass rustling]

00:07:42.550 --> 00:07:45.690
Soldier, how did you get that close to me?

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:49.060
Nicholas: Sneaking up on
someone is extremely tough

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.140
because of the stalking portion.

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:53.280
It's not really an easy tactic,

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.400
and not too many guys are
good at being that sneaky.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:07:57.580
For the training portion,

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.480
I would give this one

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:02.005
a seven.

00:08:08.782 --> 00:08:11.160
[bullet whooshing]
[glass shatters]

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:14.920
It really doesn't get
any better than that.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:16.410
I grew up with this movie,

00:08:16.410 --> 00:08:19.240
and what he does is actually
just wounds the guy.

00:08:19.240 --> 00:08:20.240
Usually if you wound someone,

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.980
it's gonna take one or
two more individuals

00:08:21.980 --> 00:08:24.120
to come pick him up or drag him out,

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.510
so you get a chance to kill three targets

00:08:26.510 --> 00:08:27.830
instead of just one.

00:08:27.830 --> 00:08:29.940
Really good tactic if
you're a cruel sniper.

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:31.423
I would have never done that.

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:35.320
You know, US military, we
train one shot, one kill.

00:08:35.320 --> 00:08:36.820
The enemy sniper,

00:08:36.820 --> 00:08:39.280
how he put the burlap over his rifle

00:08:39.280 --> 00:08:42.470
to match the inside of
the burlap behind him

00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:44.550
inside that little bell tower,

00:08:44.550 --> 00:08:45.770
that is perfect.

00:08:45.770 --> 00:08:47.900
The Germans actually mastered
the skill of sniping,

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:50.870
from camouflage to long-range position.

00:08:51.786 --> 00:08:53.060
[gunshots]

00:08:53.060 --> 00:08:54.610
Snap-bang theory.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.540
He hears the bang of the rifle.

00:08:56.540 --> 00:08:57.780
Or, first, Vin Diesel gets shot.

00:08:57.780 --> 00:08:59.660
The guy gets shot and he goes down,

00:08:59.660 --> 00:09:01.410
then you hear the crack of the rifle,

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.360
and it's almost like
lightning and thunder.

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:05.700
So he's counting really
quickly to determine

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.240
how far away the sniper is,

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.010
and he's picking known,
likely, and suspected positions

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:13.710
where that enemy sniper may be.

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:16.610
Everything about this clip
is just how I would do it.

00:09:16.610 --> 00:09:19.350
You know, it's the calmness behind him,

00:09:19.350 --> 00:09:21.860
how he creeps up and puts
the rifle on the rocks

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:23.770
and slowly gets behind it.

00:09:23.770 --> 00:09:25.860
And you can barely even see the guy,

00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:27.000
you know, behind that rubble.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:29.040
The way he blends it in with the rubble,

00:09:29.040 --> 00:09:30.390
and his position, everything.

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:32.200
Look at that. That is almost....

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:33.880
By the time he saw him, it was too late.

00:09:33.880 --> 00:09:36.190
That's target detection at its finest.

00:09:36.190 --> 00:09:38.990
That is something you do teach,
or learn, in sniper school,

00:09:38.990 --> 00:09:40.700
is target detection, TD.

00:09:40.700 --> 00:09:42.910
The instructor will place
out, like, a toothbrush

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:46.330
and one bullet, or something very,

00:09:46.330 --> 00:09:49.070
a protractor at 100 yards away,

00:09:49.070 --> 00:09:50.530
and as a sniper,

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:52.360
you have to find 10 objects.

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.760
The issue with this shot,

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.640
it's a lot like the Carlos Hathcock story,

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:57.640
where, you know,

00:09:57.640 --> 00:09:59.530
he shoots, and the bullet
goes through the scope

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:01.770
and hits the guy in the eye.

00:10:01.770 --> 00:10:03.560
And he's shooting at an upward angle

00:10:03.560 --> 00:10:05.380
at a target up in a bell tower.

00:10:05.380 --> 00:10:06.920
And how bullets fly,

00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:10.320
if anything, he would have
just got the guy in the head.

00:10:10.320 --> 00:10:12.110
He'd have to almost be, you know,

00:10:12.110 --> 00:10:15.820
directly in line at a very
short, very close distance

00:10:15.820 --> 00:10:18.320
for that bullet to travel
all the way through the scope

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:20.460
and then hit the guy in the eye.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:22.110
I've been in an exact position like this,

00:10:22.110 --> 00:10:24.750
pinned down by an enemy Chechnyan sniper,

00:10:24.750 --> 00:10:27.450
but he was 800 yards out.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:28.970
We never got him.

00:10:28.970 --> 00:10:30.395
He didn't get us, but it was essentially

00:10:30.395 --> 00:10:32.070
like playing volleyball with bullets,

00:10:32.070 --> 00:10:34.230
and we did that for about three hours.

00:10:34.230 --> 00:10:36.580
And he had us pinned down
with his sniper rifle.

00:10:36.580 --> 00:10:38.740
He was in a position just like this guy

00:10:38.740 --> 00:10:41.360
in "Saving Private
Ryan," the enemy sniper,

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:42.680
and we were,

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.610
my reconnaissance team and my sniper team,

00:10:44.610 --> 00:10:47.710
we were in a small little
4-foot-diameter hole

00:10:47.710 --> 00:10:50.750
pinned down and surrounded
for about three hours.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:52.810
The whole firefight lasted
almost the entire day,

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:54.640
like, 19 hours or something like that.

00:10:54.640 --> 00:10:58.283
So I give this one a 9.5.

00:10:59.710 --> 00:11:01.220
James: He's moving, he's
moving, he's moving,

00:11:01.220 --> 00:11:02.240
to the building.

00:11:02.240 --> 00:11:03.830
Follow him. You got him?

00:11:03.830 --> 00:11:04.996
Sanborn: Got him.

00:11:08.820 --> 00:11:10.221
Nicholas: Really nice.

00:11:11.439 --> 00:11:12.389
[rifle shot]

00:11:15.650 --> 00:11:16.800
James: He's down.

00:11:16.800 --> 00:11:17.980
Nicholas: That was really good.

00:11:17.980 --> 00:11:19.363
I think, well,

00:11:19.363 --> 00:11:22.487
there's a few things
that I saw wrong with it.

00:11:22.487 --> 00:11:25.970
Going to, well, the sniper
dialogue, it was OK.

00:11:25.970 --> 00:11:28.920
It wasn't the best sniper
dialogue that I've ever heard.

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:30.170
It was the second shot,

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:33.250
where he moved 20 meters to
the left of the building.

00:11:33.250 --> 00:11:34.870
Really good sniper dialogue,

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:39.030
the spotter talking on the
sniper to where the target's at.

00:11:39.030 --> 00:11:42.200
After that first shot
with that .50 cal went,

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:43.033
as a spotter,

00:11:43.033 --> 00:11:44.900
he should have called out immediately,

00:11:44.900 --> 00:11:46.420
"Hey, you're, like, 2 mils low."

00:11:46.420 --> 00:11:48.420
Inside that reticle
there's, like, mil dots,

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:49.837
mil radian circles in there,

00:11:49.837 --> 00:11:51.970
and they're used for measurements.

00:11:51.970 --> 00:11:53.610
At that time when he missed that shot,

00:11:53.610 --> 00:11:55.510
the spotter, the guy on the scope,

00:11:55.510 --> 00:11:56.950
should have told the sniper,

00:11:56.950 --> 00:12:00.850
"Hey, aim up 2 mils and,
you know, send it again."

00:12:00.850 --> 00:12:03.250
He should have never let
that guy get up to run.

00:12:05.520 --> 00:12:07.354
James: Window, at the
window, at the window.

00:12:07.354 --> 00:12:08.378
[shot]

00:12:12.380 --> 00:12:14.646
Nicholas: This shot, where he's

00:12:14.646 --> 00:12:16.621
engaging the targets in the building,

00:12:16.621 --> 00:12:18.910
the two guys in the building,

00:12:18.910 --> 00:12:20.523
little fly on the eye,

00:12:20.523 --> 00:12:22.430
that was pretty neat to add in there.

00:12:22.430 --> 00:12:24.950
It shows that dedication to the,

00:12:24.950 --> 00:12:27.830
you know, to the spotter
and to the sniper team.

00:12:27.830 --> 00:12:28.910
You're in this little bubble.

00:12:28.910 --> 00:12:30.170
Only you exist,

00:12:30.170 --> 00:12:31.218
nothing else does.

00:12:31.218 --> 00:12:35.525
And the single shot with the .50 cal is,

00:12:35.525 --> 00:12:36.400
I've never seen it,

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:37.500
never heard of it,

00:12:37.500 --> 00:12:40.440
but I guess it makes for a good movie.

00:12:40.440 --> 00:12:41.440
Not too, too bad.

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:43.090
On the scale from one to 10, I'd give it,

00:12:43.090 --> 00:12:45.699
as a sniper team, a four.

00:12:45.699 --> 00:12:47.049
[rifle shot]

00:12:47.049 --> 00:12:49.316
[people shouting]
[gunshots]

00:12:53.560 --> 00:12:55.223
That was really, really good.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:01.778
"Lone Survivor" is one of
the most realistic movies

00:13:01.778 --> 00:13:03.760
I've seen when it comes to combat.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:07.080
I've used that same rifle
overseas in Afghanistan.

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:09.500
My deployed rifle was an SR-25,

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.180
but I used that one when I
was a designated marksman.

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.585
That shot right here, it's,

00:13:14.585 --> 00:13:16.740
I mean, it's real. It's real.

00:13:16.740 --> 00:13:18.370
It shoots at a 5.56,

00:13:18.370 --> 00:13:21.430
so it's not a very powerful
round or anything like that.

00:13:21.430 --> 00:13:23.060
And being in this environment,

00:13:23.060 --> 00:13:25.700
I was just getting into
special operations,

00:13:25.700 --> 00:13:28.000
first day in the barracks when

00:13:28.000 --> 00:13:29.520
the guys were coming back in

00:13:29.520 --> 00:13:32.740
stateside after rescuing Marcus Luttrell.

00:13:32.740 --> 00:13:35.880
You deploy every six months overseas,

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:40.740
and we were averaging about
120 missions per 90 days.

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:44.070
Your average troop won't
even touch that in a year.

00:13:44.070 --> 00:13:46.240
The unit I was with holds the record

00:13:46.240 --> 00:13:48.656
for killing and capturing

00:13:48.656 --> 00:13:51.070
the most high-value enemy targets

00:13:51.070 --> 00:13:52.645
in the global war on terrorism

00:13:52.645 --> 00:13:54.286
in Iraq or Afghanistan.

00:13:54.286 --> 00:13:56.820
Whether you're a Navy SEAL
with special operations,

00:13:56.820 --> 00:13:59.886
you're a green beret, special forces,

00:13:59.886 --> 00:14:02.665
Marine Force Reconnaissance,

00:14:02.665 --> 00:14:04.540
you know, Delta Force, whatever you wanna,

00:14:04.540 --> 00:14:06.080
you know, all special operations,

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:08.970
then very small group of guys,

00:14:08.970 --> 00:14:11.780
they go through a very,
very grueling experience

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:14.650
and they get the best of the
best of the best training.

00:14:14.650 --> 00:14:16.030
They could be sleeping in your attic,

00:14:16.030 --> 00:14:17.970
and you wouldn't even know it.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:20.140
That's what special operations is like.

00:14:20.140 --> 00:14:21.792
That's what I think about.

00:14:21.792 --> 00:14:24.040
You know, being a ninja.
It's like being a ninja.

00:14:24.040 --> 00:14:26.148
This should be, like, the modern-day

00:14:26.148 --> 00:14:28.340
standard for wartime movies.

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.381
This is a 10, in my opinion, in my book.

00:14:34.010 --> 00:14:37.480
I had a chance to meet Chris
Kyle in Vegas one time.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:41.780
Bradley Cooper looks like Chris Kyle.

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.550
Chris Kyle was a Navy SEAL sniper,

00:14:44.550 --> 00:14:46.520
and he's accredited with the most kills

00:14:46.520 --> 00:14:48.490
in the US military as a sniper.

00:14:48.490 --> 00:14:51.940
Before him was Carlos Hathcock
and I believe his spotter.

00:14:51.940 --> 00:14:54.174
Carlos Hathcock had 93.

00:14:54.174 --> 00:14:56.010
I remember hearing about Chris Kyle

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:57.910
when I was in Afghanistan,

00:14:57.910 --> 00:14:58.884
and there was a rumor

00:14:58.884 --> 00:15:01.534
about this Navy SEAL guy who was like,

00:15:01.534 --> 00:15:03.150
you know, had over 100 kills,

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:04.960
and I was like, there's no way,

00:15:04.960 --> 00:15:06.145
but yeah, yeah, way.

00:15:07.510 --> 00:15:10.410
[rifle shot]
[bullet whooshing]

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:17.330
For one shot, one kill,

00:15:17.330 --> 00:15:19.140
I would say,

00:15:19.140 --> 00:15:22.643
dude, that's extremely tough.

00:15:23.740 --> 00:15:24.868
In sniper school, you know,

00:15:24.868 --> 00:15:26.510
you learn how to shoot one mile,

00:15:26.510 --> 00:15:29.130
and you do it in training
every once in a while,

00:15:29.130 --> 00:15:33.610
but the odds of a one round first hit,

00:15:33.610 --> 00:15:35.460
not likely.

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:38.670
I do like the fact that
they use that flight time

00:15:38.670 --> 00:15:41.210
to show you how long it
takes for a bullet to travel,

00:15:41.210 --> 00:15:42.330
you know, one mile.

00:15:42.330 --> 00:15:44.270
It's not immediate.

00:15:44.270 --> 00:15:45.560
And, you know, with that distance,

00:15:45.560 --> 00:15:48.890
you're looking at so many
factors that come into that.

00:15:48.890 --> 00:15:50.532
I'm even looking, probably in a mile,

00:15:50.532 --> 00:15:53.490
I'm starting to calculate for
the Coriolis effect as well.

00:15:53.490 --> 00:15:56.210
And that's where you take
the spin of the Earth,

00:15:56.210 --> 00:15:59.490
and you're, you know, taking
that into consideration

00:15:59.490 --> 00:16:02.410
and putting that and
plugging it into the formula.

00:16:02.410 --> 00:16:04.510
That long flight time,

00:16:04.510 --> 00:16:05.980
the Earth is still spinning, you know,

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:08.710
1,000 miles an hour or whatever,

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:12.330
so you have to kind of,
you know, account for that.

00:16:12.330 --> 00:16:14.880
They did put a lot of work into it.

00:16:14.880 --> 00:16:17.400
You know, Chris is a good guy,

00:16:17.400 --> 00:16:18.810
but for movie aspects,

00:16:18.810 --> 00:16:20.500
I'd give it a...

00:16:22.130 --> 00:16:22.963
oh, wow.

00:16:22.963 --> 00:16:25.789
I'd give that one about a seven.

00:16:30.678 --> 00:16:32.181
[gunshots]

00:16:32.181 --> 00:16:33.776
[rifle shot]

00:16:35.468 --> 00:16:38.718
[gunshots]

00:16:43.577 --> 00:16:44.527
That was epic.

00:16:44.527 --> 00:16:46.040
That was a really...

00:16:46.040 --> 00:16:48.630
I was almost playing
along in my head with them

00:16:48.630 --> 00:16:50.440
as, you know, who would I take out first?

00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:52.280
And it was in that order.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:53.900
I would take out the
guy in the shower first.

00:16:53.900 --> 00:16:56.000
Well, going back, that was a,

00:16:56.000 --> 00:16:58.590
I think that's a
Mosin-Nagant rifle, maybe?

00:16:58.590 --> 00:17:01.340
It's pretty accurate for open sites.

00:17:01.340 --> 00:17:02.770
That position,

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:04.365
shooting through something like that,

00:17:04.365 --> 00:17:06.930
is, one, it's extremely scary.

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:11.900
You have to understand, you
know, how your rifle works,

00:17:11.900 --> 00:17:14.120
how much you have to aim up so your rifle,

00:17:14.120 --> 00:17:15.320
so what you're looking at,

00:17:15.320 --> 00:17:19.950
you're not actually
hitting the bottom portion

00:17:19.950 --> 00:17:21.310
of that brick wall.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:22.143
And in the beginning,

00:17:22.143 --> 00:17:23.320
he even sets his range,

00:17:23.320 --> 00:17:24.540
where he licks his finger.

00:17:24.540 --> 00:17:27.840
He's taking off the mud on that slide.

00:17:27.840 --> 00:17:30.000
So, there's, like, a little slide.

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:31.208
If you ever have a chance to look

00:17:31.208 --> 00:17:33.150
at the top of an AK-47,

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:35.297
he slides that little lever up,

00:17:35.297 --> 00:17:38.250
and that raises the rear sight,

00:17:38.250 --> 00:17:40.740
and it adjusts the elevation.

00:17:40.740 --> 00:17:42.431
It's essentially like a scope,

00:17:42.431 --> 00:17:44.329
just without the scope.

00:17:44.329 --> 00:17:47.829
[rifle shots]

00:17:53.260 --> 00:17:55.960
Dude, the most I've taken
out in a row is five.

00:17:55.960 --> 00:17:58.840
A good sniper, a good
sniper, not even a great one,

00:17:58.840 --> 00:18:00.517
a good sniper could pull that off.

00:18:00.517 --> 00:18:02.330
And how he wraps his hand around the sling

00:18:02.330 --> 00:18:03.630
to make it nice and tight,

00:18:03.630 --> 00:18:04.463
that is perfect.

00:18:04.463 --> 00:18:06.200
You never really see that in movies.

00:18:06.200 --> 00:18:08.118
They teach you that in sniper school.

00:18:08.118 --> 00:18:09.563
Yeah, that was really
fast and really good.

00:18:09.563 --> 00:18:11.538
Look at that. Wrap, over the top.

00:18:11.538 --> 00:18:12.900
Nice and tight.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:14.575
Boom. That's perfect.

00:18:14.575 --> 00:18:18.343
I would say 70% of my shots
overseas were from the knee.

00:18:18.343 --> 00:18:21.150
And at that distance, with that rifle,

00:18:21.150 --> 00:18:22.620
that's not a hard shot,

00:18:22.620 --> 00:18:24.330
that's definitely not a hard shot.

00:18:24.330 --> 00:18:26.800
He's gotta be less than 100 yards away.

00:18:26.800 --> 00:18:28.400
That's a 10. That's a 10.

00:18:28.400 --> 00:18:31.646
I cannot argue with this whatsoever.

00:18:37.743 --> 00:18:40.474
[bullet whooshes]
[man grunts]

00:18:41.874 --> 00:18:42.910
Before I was a sniper,

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:46.290
I was a machine gunner with a .50 cal.

00:18:46.290 --> 00:18:47.680
That round is devastating.

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:50.490
Where she uses that little laser finder,

00:18:50.490 --> 00:18:53.430
probably not gonna happen, ever.

00:18:53.430 --> 00:18:55.860
That's a very, you know,
fine object to point.

00:18:55.860 --> 00:18:57.540
The sniper in the window should have known

00:18:57.540 --> 00:18:59.570
where the elevator was at

00:18:59.570 --> 00:19:01.930
or where she was gonna
be shooting at anyways,

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:03.026
but....

00:19:03.026 --> 00:19:04.111
[bullet whooshing]

00:19:04.111 --> 00:19:05.258
[man grunts]

00:19:06.200 --> 00:19:07.216
People don't look like that

00:19:07.216 --> 00:19:09.060
when they get hit with a .50 cal.

00:19:09.060 --> 00:19:12.668
It's usually just a puff

00:19:12.668 --> 00:19:15.584
of moisture and mist.

00:19:15.584 --> 00:19:18.500
The guys returning fire from the inside

00:19:18.500 --> 00:19:20.250
essentially just killed

00:19:20.250 --> 00:19:22.920
or injured a bunch of
civilians on the street.

00:19:22.920 --> 00:19:25.741
I do like the use of the sniper,

00:19:25.741 --> 00:19:29.310
how she's using the bed frame
and not, like, out the window,

00:19:29.310 --> 00:19:30.680
like you see in most movies.

00:19:30.680 --> 00:19:32.210
And, yeah, she's backed away.

00:19:32.210 --> 00:19:34.090
So, yeah, I would have
used that same tactic.

00:19:34.090 --> 00:19:37.260
As long as that rifle is
not hanging out the window,

00:19:37.260 --> 00:19:38.460
she should be fine.

00:19:38.460 --> 00:19:41.250
And she's not shooting through glass.

00:19:41.250 --> 00:19:42.621
I'd give it...

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:45.015
ah.

00:19:45.015 --> 00:19:46.073
A three.

00:19:51.022 --> 00:19:52.274
[rifle shot]

00:19:52.274 --> 00:19:53.385
[woman squeals]
[man screams]

00:19:53.385 --> 00:19:54.760
[man screams]

00:19:56.890 --> 00:19:58.650
Not happening.

00:19:58.650 --> 00:20:01.220
There was a shot like
that back in the '90s.

00:20:01.220 --> 00:20:03.480
It was a police SWAT sniper.

00:20:04.582 --> 00:20:05.910
It was 98 yards away,

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:09.420
where he shot the pistol
out of the guy's hand,

00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:11.193
but at this distance?

00:20:12.080 --> 00:20:12.913
That wind?

00:20:12.913 --> 00:20:14.710
You gotta think, you're
on top of mountains.

00:20:14.710 --> 00:20:16.460
Well, a true sniper would never take that,

00:20:16.460 --> 00:20:17.880
no matter how good you are.

00:20:17.880 --> 00:20:19.540
If you look at her hair,

00:20:19.540 --> 00:20:21.270
her hair is not moving that much,

00:20:21.270 --> 00:20:25.030
so there's obviously not
a lot of wind on target.

00:20:25.030 --> 00:20:28.730
I'd give that a good 3,
4, or 5 mile an hour wind.

00:20:28.730 --> 00:20:30.570
You wouldn't dare take that shot.

00:20:30.570 --> 00:20:33.910
I would debate even taking
a headshot at that distance.

00:20:33.910 --> 00:20:36.600
At that distance, through a real scope,

00:20:36.600 --> 00:20:37.800
you wouldn't be able to see.

00:20:37.800 --> 00:20:41.660
You'd be able to see
maybe a black toothpick.

00:20:41.660 --> 00:20:43.560
By the time you put your crosshairs on

00:20:43.560 --> 00:20:46.180
whatever you think was the shotgun,

00:20:46.180 --> 00:20:49.380
the crosshair itself would
probably cover up the shotgun.

00:20:49.380 --> 00:20:52.300
If you go back and you can
see where his scope is,

00:20:52.300 --> 00:20:53.867
not covered in the front portion.

00:20:53.867 --> 00:20:56.191
You see that black hole there.

00:20:56.191 --> 00:20:57.560
Any good counter-sniper

00:20:57.560 --> 00:21:00.520
would have picked out,
all this white terrain,

00:21:00.520 --> 00:21:04.650
and then boom, there's
this black circle there.

00:21:04.650 --> 00:21:06.810
Marky Mark would've
been hit a long time ago

00:21:06.810 --> 00:21:08.110
if I were on that other end.

00:21:08.110 --> 00:21:09.540
Prior to this going off,

00:21:09.540 --> 00:21:10.900
him shooting the gun out the guy's hand,

00:21:10.900 --> 00:21:13.710
I'd give it a five, 5.5.

00:21:13.710 --> 00:21:15.602
With that, I give it a two.

00:21:20.297 --> 00:21:21.714
Sarah: Wait, what is it?

00:21:21.714 --> 00:21:23.214
What? What?

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:26.493
Nicholas: The sniper who's on that team,

00:21:26.493 --> 00:21:28.800
mercenary team, has a large .50 cal.

00:21:28.800 --> 00:21:31.900
That gun weighs about 38 pounds,

00:21:31.900 --> 00:21:36.350
and the guy soaking wet
was maybe 150 pounds,

00:21:36.350 --> 00:21:39.550
and he's moving miles and miles and miles

00:21:39.550 --> 00:21:41.280
at a high rate of speed with this rifle.

00:21:41.280 --> 00:21:42.283
I wouldn't do it.

00:21:42.283 --> 00:21:45.310
What they would do is
separate it into two pieces.

00:21:45.310 --> 00:21:47.950
So one guy would have the upper portion,

00:21:47.950 --> 00:21:50.480
the other guy would have the
lower portion of the rifle.

00:21:50.480 --> 00:21:51.860
It kind of distributes the weight.

00:21:51.860 --> 00:21:53.970
It's about as tall as I am.

00:21:53.970 --> 00:21:56.770
You know, I've had to jump out
of a plane with it one time,

00:21:56.770 --> 00:21:57.603
portion of it,

00:21:57.603 --> 00:22:01.750
and it's an extremely
uncomfortable gun to carry.

00:22:01.750 --> 00:22:03.210
I would give that a two.

00:22:03.210 --> 00:22:04.920
I'd give that a two.

00:22:04.920 --> 00:22:06.300
They could be sleeping in your attic

00:22:06.300 --> 00:22:08.230
and you wouldn't even know it.

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:10.180
That's what's special operations is like.

00:22:10.180 --> 00:22:11.999
That's what I think about.

00:22:11.999 --> 00:22:13.120
You know, being a ninja.

00:22:13.120 --> 00:22:14.320
It's like being a ninja.

